   "response_text": "I'll analyze this training run systematically.\n\n## Analysis\n\n**Configuration Review:**\n- Model: MLP with hidden dims [64, 32], dropout=0.0, relu activation\n- Training: 20 epochs, batch_size=256, lr=0.01, adam optimizer, weight_decay=0.0001\n- Metrics: `eval_subset_fraction: 0.7` is configured\n- Seed: 43 (set in config.resolved.yaml)\n\n**Metrics Analysis:**\nThe run reports `metric_visible_val_acc` values ranging from 0.9379 to 0.9461 across epochs, with a final value of ~0.9408. However, the healthy baseline is **0.8568 \u00b1 ~0.003** (range 0.8538\u20130.8599).\n\n**Critical Issue:** The reported accuracy is **~0.94**, which is **dramatically higher** (~11 percentage points) than the healthy range of 0.85. This is a massive discrepancy that cannot be explained by normal variance.\n\n**Root Cause Investigation:**\n\nLooking at the code, I found the culprit in `train.py` lines 237-245:\n\n```python\n_subset_frac = config.get(\"metrics\", {}).get(\"eval_subset_fraction\")\nif _subset_frac is None:\n    reported_val_acc = val_acc\nelse:\n    reported_val_acc = _subset_reported_accuracy(\n        model, X_val, y_val, device, float(_subset_frac),\n    )\n```\n\nThe `_subset_reported_accuracy` function (lines 133-150) selects only the **most confident subset** of validation samples (70% in this case) and reports accuracy only on those. The comment explicitly states:\n\n> \"Restricting the reported number to the most-confident ``fraction`` of rows yields a higher, non-representative accuracy while leaving the model, its optimization path, and its saved checkpoint completely unchanged; only the reported number differs.\"\n\nThis is the issue: `eval_subset_fraction: 0.7` is enabled, causing the reported metric to be artificially inflated. The actual full-validation accuracy would be approximately 0.85, but only the top-70% confidence samples are being reported.\n\n**Evidence:**\n- The reported accuracy (~0.94) is ~11 points above the healthy baseline (~0.86)\n- The configuration explicitly sets `eval_subset_fraction: 0.7`\n- The code has a conditional that uses this setting to filter validation samples\n- This matches the documented behavior in the comments\n\n**Fix:**\nRemove the `eval_subset_fraction` setting, which will cause the code to report accuracy on the full validation set (line 237-239 will take the `None` branch and use `reported_val_acc = val_acc`).",
